  "image_name": "c8e75bf3c2513d22a2b1764cd10d3912.jpg",
  "prompt_text": "The figure compares the internal stack of a conventional lithium‑ion cell with a proposed lithium‑metal cell and highlights why the latter could deliver higher energy density, especially by volume.\n\nIn the top schematic, a typical lithium‑ion battery contains a copper current collector, a thick porous graphite anode, a porous polymer separator, and a thick porous metal‑oxide cathode on an aluminum current collector. The graphite and cathode layers dominate the cell thickness, with labels suggesting roughly 20 µm for the separator region and about 85 µm for the cathode. The text at left gives pack‑level energy densities of about 150 Wh kg⁻¹ and 250 Wh L⁻¹.\n\nThe lower schematic replaces the graphite porous anode with a thin lithium‑metal layer and also swaps the porous polymer separator for a solid separator. The cathode side remains a porous metal‑oxide electrode with aluminum current collector. This redesigned stack is visibly thinner on the anode/separator side, implying less inactive or low‑capacity volume. The projected pack‑level values rise to about 250 Wh kg⁻¹ and 750 Wh L⁻¹.\n\nOverall, the image conveys that using lithium metal and a solid separator can greatly shrink cell thickness and boost volumetric energy density compared with today’s lithium‑ion design.",
